course: dictionary
lesson: 9053
title: A sleepwalker, in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sleepwalker is a person who gets up and walks about, or does other things, while still asleep. The word is made of two parts you already know: sleep and walker. Context helps too: «Sleepwalking is common in children and usually harmless.» The sentence tells you it is a sleep behaviour that many children have.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Waking a sleepwalker will not kill them, though they may be confused.» The word confused tells you what a sleepwalker is like when woken: not hurt, just mixed up and unsure where they are.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it in the sentence. «Sleepwalking happens in deep sleep, not while dreaming.» Does sleepwalker still make sense if you think of someone asleep, not dreaming? Yes, so your meaning fits.
fact: A sleepwalker gets up and moves about while still asleep.
fact: «Sleepwalking» is the action, and «sleepwalker» is the person doing it.
fact: Sleepwalking happens in deep sleep, not while dreaming.
fact: Children usually grow out of sleepwalking as they get older.
fact: If you meet a sleepwalker, gently guide them back to bed; waking them is not dangerous, just confusing.
exercise: «Sleepwalking happens in deep sleep, not while dreaming.» What does a sleepwalker do? | Wakes up and goes to school | Sings loudly in the night | *Gets up and walks about while still asleep | Lies still and dreams quietly
exercise: «A sleepwalker should be guided gently back to bed, not shouted at.» What should you do? | *Guide them gently back to bed | Leave them alone in the hall | Turn on every light in the house | Shout loudly to wake them up
exercise: «Sleepwalking is common in children and usually harmless.» Find and copy one word that means does no damage. | sleepwalking | children | common | *harmless
test: «Waking a sleepwalker will not kill them, though they may be confused.» What may happen to a sleepwalker who is woken? | They turn into a dream | They must stay awake all night | *They may be confused | They are seriously hurt
test: «Children usually grow out of it.» What does this mean? | Sleepwalking only happens in the daytime | *Sleepwalking usually stops as they get older | Sleepwalking never stops | Sleepwalking gets worse as they get older
exam: Mo says a sleepwalker is someone who is dreaming very hard. Is Mo right? | Yes, a sleepwalker is awake and dreaming | Yes, dreams make people walk about | *No, sleepwalking happens in deep sleep, not while dreaming | No, a sleepwalker is awake and walking
